The spec is complete. I created three documents under `.kiro/specs/citydex-core/`:

**requirements.md** — 15 numbered requirements as EARS-style user stories with granular acceptance criteria (REQ-n.m). Covers all P0 features (scaffold/demo guarantee, map, check-in, AI card, Dex, demo health provider, points/leaderboard), P1 features (badges, goals, recommendations, friends/feed, Strava + Apple Health export), and the full privacy block (REQ-14: consent, EXIF strip, person-photo rejection, no location on leaderboards, token encryption, RLS, account deletion) as hard requirements. Each criterion uses WHEN/WHILE/IF-THEN/WHERE + THE SYSTEM SHALL.

**design.md** — architecture diagram and layering rules, config/demo-mode resolution, the full data model with RLS, the demo-mode fallback storage design (a single `Store` interface with `supabaseStore` and `memoryStore` implementations so route handlers never branch on backend), the `HealthProvider`/`HealthSnapshot` adapter with merge rules, the AI card pipeline (pointing to the `claude-vision-card` skill rather than guessing SDK shapes), the numbered check-in pipeline sequence, and the points/badges/recommendation/geo algorithms expressed as pure functions. A privacy-enforcement map ties each REQ-14 criterion to a mechanism.

**tasks.md** — 62 checkbox tasks grouped M0→M8, each referencing requirement IDs and each small enough for one agent turn, with explicit lint/typecheck/test gates and commit messages per milestone (no `Co-Authored-By`). Human-only items (creating accounts/keys) are flagged with the demo-fallback-and-continue instruction.

I followed the workflow rule's milestone ordering, wired privacy requirements in as non-negotiable, and kept pure logic isolated for testability per the structure steering. No application code was written. One thing to flag: the steering references a `claude-vision-card` skill while AGENT_PROMPT.md calls it `claude-api` — I used the name from the available skills list (`claude-vision-card`) in the spec, since that's the one actually present.
